                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "The first Fallout game holds up well even after nearly 30 years. This was my introduction to CRPGs despite Fallout 3 being my introduction to RPGs as a whole (and Fallout itself). The story, setting, characters and locations are all extremely interesting to explore and see. The retro futuristic, yet believable setting truly creates a unique world to explore. In addition with the post apocalyptic and some light horror elements, the world is almost like no other game.\n\nThe character creation allows for nearly any kind of character to be created and every choice feels like they have an impact. The quests are interesting and engaging with multiple ways to complete them, the turn based gameplay with the VATS system allows for more tactics to be used and the dialogue you can have with the characters is quite varied and deep which really immerses you into the world.\n\nBut I believe that where the game truly exceeds in a perfect manner is the presentation. This is the most serious of the Fallout games, and not just that. The presentation with the art style of the graphics and the HUD are very industrial looking, bleak and hopeless, conveying a feeling of a world that once was. The talking heads are crafted in a very uncanny but detailed manner. All of this is accompanied with an OST that is some of the most amazing ambient music I've heard in a game and you have a perfect mix to immerse you while you blast your enemies into a bloody mess and constantly check if you are irradiated.\n\nThe biggest issue with this game is the time limit in the beginning for the main quest. You don't have much time to explore in the beginning. There are also some bugs such as lag which do hinder the experience a little. Other than that everything is near perfectly executed.\n\n9/10",
